        "title" : null,
        "hasTitle" : false,
        "message" : [
          "%md\r",
          "\r",
          "***Goal***\r",
          "\r",
          "This notebook uses Oracle Machine Learning for Python (OML4Py) to segment bank accounts based on the **already normalized** graph metrics in `BANK_GRAPH_FEATURES_NORMALIZED`.\r",
          "\r",
          "It trains two K-Means models, assigns each account to behaviour groups, and saves the results from the five clusters for use in the enriched clustered graph.\r",
          "**Questions**\r",
          "\r",
          "-How can we divide all accounts into five reusable behaviour segments?\r",
          "-Which accounts have similar cycle behaviour, and which have similar importance behaviour across the whole network?\r",
          "In this notebook, it groups every account using cycle metrics or centrality metrics.\r",
          "\r",
          "**You will learn how to:**\r",
          "\r",
          "- Load `BANK_GRAPH_FEATURES_NORMALIZED` from Oracle Database.\r",
          "- Train a cycle-behaviour K-Means model using `CYCLE_2_NORM`, `CYCLE_3_NORM`, and `CYCLE_4_NORM`.\r",
          "- Train an importance K-Means model using `PAGERANK_NORM`, `BETWEENNESS_NORM`, and `CLOSENESS_NORM`.\r",
          "- Review model centroids to understand the typical metric values in each group.\r",
          "- Assign a cycle cluster ID and an importance cluster ID to every account.\r",
          "- Save the combined results in `BANK_GRAPH_CLUSTER_RESULTS`.\r",
          "\r",
          "**Note:** K-Means groups accounts with similar metric values. It does not identify graph communities or calculate PageRank, betweenness, closeness, or cycles.\r"
        ],
        "selectedVisualization" : "html",
        "visualizationConfig" : null,
        "hideCode" : true,
        "hideResult" : false,
        "hideGutter" : true,
        "hideVizConfig" : false,
        "hideInIFrame" : false,
        "enabled" : true,
        "forms" : "[]",
        "result" : {
          "startTime" : 1787927702952,
          "endTime" : 1787927703032,
          "interpreter" : "md.medium",
          "taskStatus" : "SUCCESS",
          "status" : "SUCCESS",
          "results" : [
            {
              "message" : "<p><em><strong>Goal</strong></em></p>\n<p>This notebook uses Oracle Machine Learning for Python (OML4Py) to segment bank accounts based on the <strong>already normalized</strong> graph metrics in <code>BANK_GRAPH_FEATURES_NORMALIZED</code>.</p>\n<p>It trains two K-Means models, assigns each account to behaviour groups, and saves the results from the five clusters for use in the enriched clustered graph.\n<strong>Questions</strong></p>\n<p>-How can we divide all accounts into five reusable behaviour segments?\n-Which accounts have similar cycle behaviour, and which have similar importance behaviour across the whole network?\nIn this notebook, it groups every account using cycle metrics or centrality metrics.</p>\n<p><strong>You will learn how to:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Load <code>BANK_GRAPH_FEATURES_NORMALIZED</code> from Oracle Database.</li>\n<li>Train a cycle-behaviour K-Means model using <code>CYCLE_2_NORM</code>, <code>CYCLE_3_NORM</code>, and <code>CYCLE_4_NORM</code>.</li>\n<li>Train an importance K-Means model using <code>PAGERANK_NORM</code>, <code>BETWEENNESS_NORM</code>, and <code>CLOSENESS_NORM</code>.</li>\n<li>Review model centroids to understand the typical metric values in each group.</li>\n<li>Assign a cycle cluster ID and an importance cluster ID to every account.</li>\n<li>Save the combined results in <code>BANK_GRAPH_CLUSTER_RESULTS</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> K-Means groups accounts with similar metric values. It does not identify graph communities or calculate PageRank, betweenness, closeness, or cycles.</p>\n",
              "type" : "HTML"
            }
          ],
          "forms" : "[]"
        },
        "relations" : [ ],
        "dynamicFormParams" : "{}"
      },
